Subject: [PATCH] write a whole buffer at a time to the output

The reader thread writes its output (a file, stdout, an http client) in
pieces of SIZE_CHANK, 1316 bytes: the size of a UDP datagram, which a file
or a TCP socket does not need.  A 16 KB buffer takes 13 write() calls.  On
the POE-BR100-YB1 the CPU is shared with taking the TS from the tuner, and
an http client could not keep up with a terrestrial multiplex (about
2.06 MB/s): writing 16356 instead of 1316 bytes a time raised what the
device sends while receiving from 2.38 to 2.89 MB/s.

Write all of a buffer at once.  A partial write is still taken care of by
the loop.  UDP keeps its datagram size.

---
diff --git a/recpt1/recpt1.c b/recpt1/recpt1.c
index e39ab4d..5a43a7a 100644
--- a/recpt1/recpt1.c
+++ b/recpt1/recpt1.c
@@ -419,8 +419,12 @@ reader_func(void *p)
             int size_remain = buf.size;
             int offset = 0;
 
+            /* All of it at once: SIZE_CHANK is the size of a UDP datagram
+             * (below), and a file or a TCP socket takes any size.  On a slow
+             * CPU the many small writes are what keeps an http client from
+             * keeping up with the tuner. */
             while(size_remain > 0) {
-                int ws = size_remain < SIZE_CHANK ? size_remain : SIZE_CHANK;
+                int ws = size_remain;
 
                 wc = write(wfd, buf.data + offset, ws);
                 if(wc < 0) {
